## DESCRIPTION
## Nonlinear systems
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('Calculus - multivariable')
## DBchapter('Differentiation of multivariable functions')
## DBsection('Nonlinear systems')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "MathObjects.pl");

TEXT(beginproblem());

BEGIN_PGML


Linear systems will often inform your intuition for nonlinear systems, so you will need a solid foundation. Suppose you are given a (homogenous) _linear_ system of equations with coefficient matrix [`A`].

There is always at least one solution [` x \in \mathbb{R}^N `] to the equation [`Ax = 0 `]. Identify that solution: [` x = `][________]{Real(0)}.

The set of solutions is given by null[` (A) = \{x \in \mathbb{R}^N: Ax = 0\} `], the null space of [`A`]. It is a subspace of [`\mathbb{R}^N`]. Assume the entries of your matrix are _randomly chosen_. For each case below, what would you expect to be the value of the dimension of null[`(A)`]?

a) The system has [`7`] linear equations and [`23`] variables, so [`A`] is a [`7 \times 23`] matrix. [________]{Real(16)}

b) The system has [`23`] linear equations and [`7`] variables, so [`A`] is a [`23 \times 7`] matrix. [________]{Real(0)}

c) The system has [`23`] linear equations and [`23`] variables, so [`A`] is a [`23 \times 23`] matrix. [________]{Real(0)}
END_PGML

$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;

ENDDOCUMENT();